# Blackwell Field Propulsion Stress-Test Engine
### Designer: C. Matthew Blackwell  
### System Class: Field Propulsion Substrate Simulation  
### Dataset: Kepler-16 (AB) Binary Star System (NASA Exoplanet Archive)

---

## Overview
This repository contains a full cinematic simulation engine that renders a
field-propulsion substrate undergoing multi-phase stress testing inside the
Kepler-16 (AB) barycentric gravitational potential.

The engine visualizes:
- Binary star orbital motion  
- Substrate gravitational field contours  
- Propulsion-induced distortion fields  
- Sapphire exoskeleton geometry  
- Dynamic fin deformation  
- Kernel telemetry (Δχ, α/β/γ matrix, thermal load, deflection)  
- Regime transitions:
  - Laminar Acceleration  
  - Adaptive Harmonic Strain  
  - Turbulent Edge Limit  
  - Emergency Decoupling  

The final output is a 60 FPS MP4 cinematic render.

---

## Features
- Real astrophysical constants from NASA Exoplanet Archive  
- Full blueprint-style rendering environment  
- Multi-regime substrate stress physiology  
- Real-time telemetry HUD  
- FFMPEG-based video export  
- Clean architectural grid aesthetic  
- Sapphire exoskeleton + dynamic fin overlays  
- Contour-based field visualization  

---

## Requirements
Install dependencies:


pip install -r requirements.txt


You must also have **FFMPEG installed** on your system PATH.

---

## Running the Simulation
Use the CLI wrapper:


python run_simulation.py


This will:
- Initialize the simulation engine  
- Render all frames  
- Compile the MP4  
- Output: `Blackwell_Field_Propulsion_Stress_Test.mp4`

